he specific file to create, then defining the core requirements around implementing the various counting flags that the wc utility needs to support. Right-justify numeric columns to match GNU wc's field-width behavior. Now I'm looking at the acceptance criteria—the binary needs to compile cleanly, handle single and multiple files correctly, support character and line-length counting flags, read from stdin when needed, and gracefully handle missing files without stopping processing.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether to include the `--files0-from` flag since GNU wc supports it, but without the PRD details I'll defer that to the stitch agent. There's also a constraint about staying within 4 PRD sub-requirements, and with 6 task requirements potentially mapping across different sub-requirements, I might be pushing the limit—though I can't verify this without seeing the actual PRD content.\n\nLooking at how the wc PRD is likely structured, it probably organizes requirements into groups like counting operations, input handling, output formatting, and exit codes, each with multiple sub-requirements. If my task touches all the major counting modes, both stdin and file inputs, the output formatting rules, and error handling, I could easily be covering a dozen or more PRD sub-requirements, which would exceed the constraint.\n\nBut wc is fundamentally a simple utility—probably just 150-200 lines total—so splitting it artificially into multiple tasks feels wrong. The constraint about the 4 sub-requirement limit seems designed for complex utilities with sprawling PRDs, not something this straightforward. I think the better approach is to scope my task more tightly: focus on the core counting logic and standard output format, and defer things like the --files0-from flag and various edge cases to a follow-up task. Let me check the exact wording of the constraint again to make sure I'm interpreting it correctly.\n\nThe constraint is clear—each task can have at most 4 sub-requirements. Since I can only propose one task, I need to pick the 4 most essential features: line counting, word counting, byte counting, and reading from both stdin and files. That gives wc its fundamental utility. I can leave character counting, max line length, and the totals line for later tasks. Though I'm second-guessing myself on whether omitting the totals feature for multiple files is too much of a cut—that's pretty basic functionality.\n\nLet me think about this differently. Maybe the PRD groups requirements hierarchically, so I could satisfy the constraint by picking one requirement group like \"core counting\" plus a few specific sub-requirements for input handling, output formatting, and error handling. That way I'm covering the essentials without violating the 4 sub-requirement limit. The key is focusing on the default output behavior and basic exit codes rather than trying to cram in every counting mode. I'm laying out the required reading materials and the main file to create, then defining the core requirements: implementing the three counting flags (-l, -w, -c), defaulting to all three when none are specified, handling file and stdin input with support for multiple files and totals, and setting proper exit codes.\n\nThe design approach is to keep everything in a single main.go file following project conventions, stick to Go's standard library, and match GNU wc's right-justified numeric columns. The acceptance criteria verify compilation, correct counting behavior, stdin support, and the remaining test cases.\n\nI'm noticing a gap though—I only have 4 requirements but the guidelines call for 5-8. The constraint about \"at most 4 PRD sub-requirements\" refers to how many distinct PRD sections I'm covering, not the total requirement count itself, so I can expand the requirements list without violating that limit. Defining acceptance criteria... I've got a solid structure here with the requirements, acceptance criteria, and design decisions all in good shape. The t